[Music]  we at the future of life institute asked  what the world might look like between  now and 2045 if we build advanced  artificial intelligence  and steer away from disaster  towards the future we actually want  we had an amazing response to the  competition with 144 submissions from 44  countries and we loved reading about  these worlds and we're really impressed  by the effort and the quality  to enter the competition participants  were asked to submit four components  a timeline of events from today to 2045  two short stories  answers to a series of prompts about  their world and a media piece to give  flavor  to their world  and all of these components had to tie  together in a coherent fashion and we  were looking for worlds that were both  plausible and aspirational and this was  really hard  there were a ton of great entries and  the judges were very diverse in their  backgrounds and opinions  even when they could agree on what was  technically plausible or implausible  which wasn't always  they often disagreed about just what was  their favorite what world would they  want to inhabit  what story was most compelling to them  etc and so this is part of the reason we  wanted to bring more voices in and have  more feedback we want to have as many  people included in the discussion as we  possibly can  um there were a few worlds that were so  aspirational like i mean i was overjoyed  after reading them  but i couldn't really quite point how to  get from here to there  and then you know vice versa there were  a few that were like scarily realistic  you know it was really hard to see how  that is actually like a future that one  wants to inhabit something challenge  obviously to strike a balance between  these different tradeoffs  after much deliberation the judges have  finalized their choices  on behalf of the future of life  institute i present to you the winners  of the 2022 world building contest  i have a sort of background in writing  uh rational fiction  which is where  you sort of take these bizarre  world-building premises and then you  find a way that that could make sense  and i don't know why that's so  interesting but it was a lot of fun for  us this was really a great project so  this is a really nice excuse to sit and  have these discussions together and the  complexity comes from so many different  minds and debates and arguments that we  had a lot of fun to kind of bring  together a bunch of ideas that we had  about like what  seems most exciting about the future and  then kind of pull those together under  you know into a coherent ish scenario  making a particular path really vivid  is  probably necessary to  enable people to believe in it there's a  shocking dearth of any kind of  optimistic  sci-fi  uh in um on the small live screens oh my  god super hard to think about  aspirational futures we just like it was  really difficult like what is what is  the aspiration  now that the contest is over we're  working on showcasing these worlds and  connecting them with storytellers  just recently the world builds were on  display at an event in los angeles put  on by hollywood health and society where  they announced their screenwriting  contest so that's something that our  organization tries to do is how to  connect technologists with people who  want to tell stories and so  we realized that like navigating  technology as a plot device can be kind  of overwhelming and i guess the hope is  that more visions of positive futures  and popular media can inspire and help  us achieve them  there's not a lot of that sort of  conversation going on  in society and nfl i wanted to help  jumpstart it  how do we get more positive visions of  the world how do we get conversation  going about what sorts of worlds we  would actually want to live in  through media through discussions  through  public debate  how can we start that process so that we  can as a society have a little bit more  of a positive goal rather than just  avoiding the negative

